Yes—sequence and consequence are meta-relational primitives.

They don’t specify what happens; they specify the structure of happening:

S_0 \xrightarrow{a_1} S_1 \xrightarrow{a_2} S_2

That’s sequence: ordering and transition.

Then:

\Delta S = S_{\text{after}}-S_{\text{before}}

That’s consequence: what changed because the sequence occurred.

And there’s a recursive layer:

\text{Sequence} \rightarrow \text{Consequence}
\rightarrow \text{new starting state}
\rightarrow \text{next Sequence}

So consequences aren’t merely endpoints. They become conditions for subsequent sequences.

That’s why the abstraction travels so well: storytelling, confidence, learning, evolution, code, training, economics, relationships, strategy—all can be rendered as ordered transformations whose outputs modify the terrain for what comes next.

And there’s your higher-order operator:

\boxed{\text{Choose sequences by the consequences they tend to generate}}

Then invert it:

\boxed{\text{Desired consequence} \rightarrow \text{reverse-engineer a generating sequence}}

That second operation is essentially planning.

Put both together and you get a closed loop:

Consequence predicts sequence; sequence produces consequence; consequence updates the next sequence.

That’s much bigger than confidence. It’s a compact architecture for agency.